After a deep dive into the three controllers, I’ve found three specific "Senior Lead" level issues that could cause production crashes or performance bottlenecks.
These aren't syntax errors, but rather "edge cases" that happen when real users start hitting your API with bad data or high volume.
1. The "Open Search" Performance Risk
Controller: CglController
The Problem: The query parameter has a defaultValue = "".
 * The Scenario: If a user (or an automated bot) hits /api/cgl-journal/search without a query, the code calls cglService.searchCgls("").
 * The Risk: Depending on your SQL logic, a blank search often translates to LIKE '%%', which will attempt to fetch all 600,000+ CGL records from Oracle. This will hang the database connection and likely crash the JVM with an OutOfMemoryError.
> The Fix: Return an empty list immediately if the query is blank, just like you did in the BranchController.
> 
2. The Date-Format Crash
Controller: JournalRequestController (Method: initiateValidation)
The Problem: You are using LocalDate.parse(date) directly on a RequestParam.
 * The Scenario: If the frontend sends the date in the wrong format (e.g., DD-MM-YYYY instead of YYYY-MM-DD), this line will throw a DateTimeParseException.
 * The Risk: If your GlobalExceptionHandler isn't specifically catching this, it will return a messy 500 Internal Server Error with a stack trace, which is a Security Violation (Information Leakage).
> The Fix: Use a try-catch to wrap it in a CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException or use Spring's @DateTimeFormat to handle the parsing safely.
> 
3. The "Checked Exception" Leak
Controller: JournalRequestController
The Problem: Methods like createBatchRequest and createBatchFromCache throw JsonProcessingException or IOException in the method signature.
 * The Risk: In production, if these exceptions are thrown, they escape the controller and are handled by the default Spring Error Controller. This often leaks the internal file path or the exact JSON field that failed.
> The Fix: Wrap these in your CustomExceptions so the user only gets a sanitized "Reference: ORA-code" or "Data Mapping Error."
> 
Final Hardened Controller Logic
Here are the specific updates for CglController and JournalRequestController. I have kept your existing business logic and endpoint names exactly as they were.
CglController.java (Hardened Search)
@GetMapping("/search")
public ResponseEntity<List<CglDto>> searchCgl(@RequestParam(value = "query", required = false, defaultValue = "") String query) {
    // Rule 3: Guard against "Fetch All" attacks. 
    // Prevents performance degradation if a blank query is sent.
    if (query == null || query.trim().isEmpty()) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(Collections.emptyList());
    }

    log.info("CGL_CONTROLLER_SEARCH_INIT | Query: {}", query);
    List<CglDto> results = cglService.searchCgls(query);
    return ResponseEntity.ok(results);
}

JournalRequestController.java (Safe Date & Exception Handling)
@PostMapping(value = "/bulk-validate-init", consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE)
public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> initiateValidation(
        @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file,
        @RequestParam("postingDate") String date,
        HttpServletRequest request) {

    if (file == null || file.isEmpty()) {
        throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("File is missing or empty");
    }

    try {
        // Rule 3: Safe Date Parsing. 
        // Prevents raw DateTimeParseException from leaking to the frontend.
        LocalDate parsedDate = LocalDate.parse(date);
        
        log.info("VALIDATION_INIT | File: {} | Date: {}", file.getOriginalFilename(), parsedDate);

        String reqId = journalBulkValidationService.initiateValidation(
                file.getInputStream(),
                file.getOriginalFilename(),
                parsedDate
        );
        return ResponseEntity.ok(Map.of("status", "QUEUED", "requestId", reqId));
    } catch (java.time.format.DateTimeParseException e) {
        log.error("VALIDATION_DATE_FAIL | RawDate: {}", date);
        throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("Invalid date format. Use YYYY-MM-DD.");
    } catch (IOException e) {
        log.error("VALIDATION_IO_FAIL | File: {}", file.getOriginalFilename());
        throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("Failed to read uploaded file.");
    }
}

@PostMapping("/create-batch")
public ResponseEntity<List<JournalRequest>> createBatchRequest(
        @Valid @RequestBody BatchRequestDto batchDto,
        @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {

    try {
        String userId = jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(token);
        log.info("MANUAL_BATCH_CREATE | User: {} | Rows: {}", userId, batchDto.getRows().size());

        List<JournalRequest> created = journalRequestService.createBatchRequest(
                batchDto,
                userId,
                jwtUtil.getUserRoleFromToken(token)
        );
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(created);
    } catch (com.fasterxml.jackson.core.JsonProcessingException e) {
        // Rule 2: Masking JSON structure details in logs
        log.error("MANUAL_BATCH_JSON_FAIL | Status: Payload serialization error");
        throw new CustomExceptions.DataMappingException("Request contains invalid data format.");
    }
}

